WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.179
GAVIN: (In progress)-in service to his country,
both as part of Namibia's struggle for independence

00:00:01.179 --> 00:00:02.179
and one of the most prominent and thoughtful
thinkers about what prosperity would look

00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:03.179
like for an independent Namibia.

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.669
And so I want to welcome him here to the Council,
to welcome him back to New York.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:09.270
And the president knows this city very, very
well, having studied at Fordham as an undergraduate,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:15.760
I believe received your master's degree in
international relations from The New School

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.060
and spent several years with the United Nations
at the Secretariat as well.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:30.740
So he knows us well and is fresh and rested
and hopefully invigorated by our beautiful

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.090
spring weather.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:37.120
And with that, I turn it over to you, Mr.
President.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.620
GEINGOB: Thank you very much.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.710
I recognize old friends like Maurice Tempelsman.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.880
Yes, we were friends for a long time and I
still see him being active.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.559
There is Kramer (sp) also, I see, and old,
old friends, you know, many of them.

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:52.149
So thank you very much.

00:00:52.149 --> 00:01:00.649
It's a great honor to be here to again exchange
views with friends and also people who advise

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:03.200
governments in policy matters and so on.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:12.119
As you know, Namibia is a young democracy
still, but indeed a true democracy in the

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:14.210
true sense of the word.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:18.090
Our governance architecture is in place.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.820
We have regular free and fair elections.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.110
Our macroeconomic architecture is also good.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.610
Our banking system is working.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:33.130
We get good ratings from rating agencies,
several B-positive but now a little bit of

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.350
some problems maybe.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:44.340
But I just want to jump to social architecture,
where we have problems because we have a good

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.670
constitution, good policies, but people now
want to benefit from those good policies.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.439
They would like to eat.

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:56.300
They would like to have accommodation-decent,
at least, accommodation, not luxury but at

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:04.420
least people want to live in something that
is livable.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:09.119
And then also the thing of peace.

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:11.489
Democracy is good.

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:17.969
It's a better system because even if you are
quarreling-I keep on saying you don't have

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:22.219
to go to war when we are in a democratic dispensation.

00:02:22.219 --> 00:02:26.099
So I tell people, yes, make noise, complain.

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:30.340
The moment you keep quiet I will know there's
something wrong.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:36.659
And that I learned from your country when
I was student during the tough days of black

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:37.659
power struggle.

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:40.290
There was war here.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:46.949
One professor at Lincoln said, people, 30
years ago were the black people better off

00:02:46.949 --> 00:02:47.949
or worse off?

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:50.249
Then we said, so what?

00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:57.010
Then he says, they were worse off but they
were so quiet; why we're not angry that time?

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.980
Then we couldn't answer that.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:06.709
He said, you know-do you know, people, when
people have hope and they see a light at the

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:09.749
end of a tunnel, they make noise.

00:03:09.749 --> 00:03:10.749
So they are hopeful.

00:03:10.749 --> 00:03:15.549
So I always use that example in my country
that people are now so angry.

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:17.889
They want things done.

00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:22.040
Sometimes I say, is it because I'm now the
president or what is it?

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.529
But then I think it's because they are hopeful.

00:03:25.529 --> 00:03:31.170
So I tell them, the moment you keep quiet
I'll be worried, so demand, demand.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.439
So we go by legacy.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:41.839
We have legacy of founding president, who
was a freedom fighter who led us in the struggle

00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:47.919
and took us back; reconciled people who were
warring, white, black, different tribes divided

00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:55.219
by bantustans, put them together, and had
a peaceful transition, building on his legacies.

00:03:55.219 --> 00:04:03.809
And then we got another president whom we
call stabilizer, a cool cat, very calm.

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:13.730
And then they say, now the person who comes
must be active and he must bring about prosperity.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.790
That's a most difficult task.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.530
And how do I do that?

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:22.879
I took all of last year with my team to kind
of have a rallying point.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:23.879
How do I wait?

00:04:23.879 --> 00:04:30.800
People 26, 25 years, some are losing hope,
so to energize them again so come up with

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.860
slogans like: building the Namibian house
where we're going to all live together peacefully

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.110
using bricks, our different tribes and races.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:45.110
It was beautifully painted, you know, and
presented and it's now the talk, Namibian

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.110
house.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.340
And I said, no one must feel left out from
that house-one Namibia.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.669
So those things have reignited.

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:53.639
Now implementation.

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:59.840
So we came up with a plan that is called Harambee
Prosperity Plan.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.880
Some say, why "harambee"?

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:02.880
That's a Swahili word.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.639
Why not our own language worked?

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:08.230
We said, yes, we have so many languages.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.790
If I were to choose my language you would
have said, oh, we are left out.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:16.960
So I took a neutral language, Swahili language,
African-but African too, so therefore Harambee,

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:23.430
meaning pulling to get in the same direction.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.520
So that's what we are now implementing.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.659
I have my experts here.

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:29.530
My economic adviser was present.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.910
I have also a group who will participate.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.600
I don't talk alone.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.240
We live in teamwork and that is the way we
do it.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.580
Everywhere I go I let them talk.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:45.280
After all, people paid for them to be here,
so they must also work so that I can say they

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:46.289
are also delivering.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:51.030
So I have the-my wife, she's also active,
a business lady.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:59.580
Then I have the deputy prime minister, who
is our foreign relations and cooperation person.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.370
Then I have the economic-minister of economic
affairs and planning.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:11.289
Then I have the minister of environment because
we are going to sign tomorrow the agreement,

00:06:11.289 --> 00:06:12.289
so he is here for that.

00:06:12.289 --> 00:06:19.770
But Namibia is a country where we have good
conservation policies so he can also pitch

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:20.770
in.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:26.770
Our two ambassadors, and Dr. Steytler, my
economic adviser, the wife of our ambassador.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.180
He used to be on my staff.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:33.139
I sent him here to Washington when I was the
minister of trade-a trade representative.

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:40.050
So I have that big team, and it will be good
that we can do that.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.050
Thank you very much.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.050
GAVIN: Absolutely.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.050
Well, thank you, Mr. President.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:47.720
I think we already get a flavor of the energy
that you're bringing to the project.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:56.020
And you have made poverty alleviation, poverty
elimination a huge priority for Namibia going

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.870
forward, but I want to draw you out on something
you and I had a chance to talk a little bit

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:06.860
about before we joined everyone.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:13.300
You're building the Namibian house collectively
with your countrymen and women, and trying

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.240
to build this prosperous future.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.129
And this is-you have tremendous human resources
in your country, clearly.

00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:26.689
You have natural resources, but help in a
building project is always a good thing, be

00:07:26.689 --> 00:07:30.660
it in the form of assistance or trade and
investment.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:36.659
And can you talk to us a little bit about
how sometimes Namibia's success and Namibia's

00:07:36.659 --> 00:07:43.240
status as upper-middle-income country can
make it challenging to access some of the

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:49.259
assistance that might make the road a bit
smoother or the journey a bit faster?

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:51.419
GEINGOB: Yes.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:57.759
I used to argue with my colleague here when
he was a banker, when he was a governor of

00:07:57.759 --> 00:08:05.930
the Bank, when I was raising that issue of
the higher-middle-income countries' status:

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.479
Why are you against being a rich country?

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:10.129
Just make it work.

00:08:10.129 --> 00:08:11.300
Yes, it's true.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:19.330
And before I say that, let me express my thanks
to President Bush administration.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:25.550
During the time that Millennium Challenge
Account was being conceived we were left out,

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:32.829
Botswana and Namibia, because we are rich,
but they made an exception to include us.

00:08:32.829 --> 00:08:40.830
And I must tell you, that's one project that
has made a mark in development in Namibia

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:46.790
when it comes to schooling, when it comes
to actually helping the conservation-excellent.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:53.209
Libraries built-you know, big libraries that
you can-people-children go and study there.

00:08:53.209 --> 00:08:56.210
You can see there is something that has been
left.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:00.770
And we were one of the countries which implemented
it about 95 percent or so, and if not more.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:05.610
So we are pleased for that, but unfortunately
it stopped.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:15.260
So that status makes it that-because of success,
it makes it that we don't get any grants or

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:18.070
soft loans and so on.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:27.170
And we came from a very bad situation-apartheid
colony where blacks were completely left out

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.220
educationally, politically, and otherwise.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:36.279
And when we come from that background we are
told, since you are rich you can't get any

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:37.279
help.

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:38.770
That is not fair.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:43.870
And if countries-I don't want to say it to
expose them, but we are making a joke out

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.170
of frustration: If you mismanage you are rewarded.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:54.949
If you have piled up debts, the debts are
written off, you get loans-soft loans, grants.

00:09:54.949 --> 00:10:00.480
But if you are successful trying to manage
your own affairs properly, they say you are

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:01.699
rich.

00:10:01.699 --> 00:10:03.440
Some members are closing down and say, we're
going to poorer countries.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:04.440
We say, wait.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:10.400
And I had to take one of them and say, are
you going to close your embassy in America-(chuckles)-because

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.899
they were trying to give (my chief ?) an excuse
to say, since you are rich we are going to

00:10:14.899 --> 00:10:16.900
a poorer country we can help.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.020
So that is not fair, really.

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:29.089
But it also made us maybe to be independent,
maybe to rely on our own resources, to maybe

00:10:29.089 --> 00:10:38.170
manage them properly and therefore to be transparent,
accountable, and depend on our own resources

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:43.470
and manage them properly, both human and material
resources.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.750
So it's true.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.600
It's not fair.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:54.840
I think for a country which came from apartheid,
white-black situation where white people are,

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.690
because of history, rich and blacks are poor
and left out.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.459
Now, I was at the World Bank for some short
time.

00:11:03.459 --> 00:11:06.649
They told me, yes, your country is rich.

00:11:06.649 --> 00:11:11.370
Your problem is that of redistribution of
that wealth.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:16.520
And I said, are you saying I must go and grab
it from the whites and give it to the blacks?

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.540
Are you going to be happy with that because
you know there is this inequality?

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:30.730
Ah, yes, maybe we are but a few countries
who have highest coefficiency-Gini coefficiency.

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:31.950
And we have brought it down.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:36.410
We are working with that, but it's still-and
you see all these countries where you have

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:39.850
this black-white situation, even the United
States.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:44.630
For an advanced country it's still high, 4-about
4, you see?

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:50.310
And we are 5 now, 5-something, but we were
6-no, we were 7.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:51.370
We brought it down.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.670
So that is what you have inherited.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:12:00.420
So although materially the country is rich
resource-wise, but when you look at people,

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:01.860
blacks-white, blacks in African countries:
poor.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:08.199
And therefore, those who are poor have help
from other countries, but we are told we are

00:12:08.199 --> 00:12:09.199
rich.

00:12:09.199 --> 00:12:10.570
And that is very serious.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:16.980
If we didn't manage Namibian reconciliation-being
a small country, we're not talk about.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.149
We came before President Mandela.

00:12:20.149 --> 00:12:27.620
South Africa whites were coming to my office
to talk to me: How did you manage it that

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:32.860
black and whites-and ours was a worse situation
because of lack of education.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:34.200
Separation was complete, so to say.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:38.930
South Africa, they were still mingling at
Cape Town University and so on at academic

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:42.850
level and so on, but ours was a complete separation.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:46.600
But we took those people-and not only white-black.

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:49.890
There's also division among our own tribes.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:53.110
The bantustans, you are this tribe, you go
there.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:13:01.579
And we took those people, put them together,
have reconciliation, and manage everything

00:13:01.579 --> 00:13:02.579
properly.

00:13:02.579 --> 00:13:09.080
And South Africa was helped by that, that
former terrorists can be trusted.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:16.970
They can create a very democratic situation
where all Namibians, all people are respected,

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:17.970
are welcome.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:19.930
That's what we are talking about.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:22.149
We are building a Namibian house.

00:13:22.149 --> 00:13:24.370
We have different ethnic groups, races.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:29.649
The bricks that we are using are our races
and colors, and laws we are passing in parliament

00:13:29.649 --> 00:13:32.839
are that glue that is putting us together.

00:13:32.839 --> 00:13:38.589
When you finish or you plaster it, you paint,
you no longer can identify individual tribes

00:13:38.589 --> 00:13:39.970
or race.

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:41.540
That's a Namibian house I'm talking about.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:46.160
That's what we are building and I think we
are making progress.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:52.199
GAVIN: That's fascinating-especially considering
how much attention there is in this political

00:13:52.199 --> 00:13:58.790
cycle here in the United States to income
inequality-to think about the experience in

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:00.089
Namibia.

00:14:00.089 --> 00:14:05.879
Now, I want to follow up on what you were
talking about in terms of the example that

00:14:05.879 --> 00:14:13.040
Namibia set for South Africa, and really a
broader question about how does a country

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:24.370
like Namibia-quite young; really by most metrics
extraordinarily successful-how does a country

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:32.600
like Namibia, though, that is small in population,
be influential regionally and globally?

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.710
Is it enough to lead by example?

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:42.620
I note, and I'm sure many others in the room
have noted, your own example.

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:46.390
You and the first lady have publicly disclosed
your assets.

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:51.560
You've insisted on your minister doing the
same.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:58.569
Your predecessor was noted for emphasis on
accountably and good governance as well, won

00:14:58.569 --> 00:15:00.759
the Ibrahim Prize.

00:15:00.759 --> 00:15:03.420
Is it enough to lead by example?

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:11.670
Or does a voice like Namibia's need to be
heard more in regional and global fora to

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:17.879
tackle some of these other problems and to
present an alternative to the vision of some

00:15:17.879 --> 00:15:22.680
countries on the continent where you do have
leaders who've been in place for decades?

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:31.370
GEINGOB: Yes, when you're a small country
it's difficult, even if you have good ideas.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:37.769
I make a joke-I made a joke once when we are
looking for a Security Council seat.

00:15:37.769 --> 00:15:40.610
I like to make jokes sometimes.

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:43.610
Then we think we must get two big countries.

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:46.000
Nigeria and South Africa are possible candidates.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:54.660
So I say when we are talking, I say, you must
also be careful; we should also think of neutral

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:58.150
smaller country because wisdom is not only
confined to big powers.

00:15:58.150 --> 00:16:04.350
We may be small but we went to the same schools,
so we may be the ones who can contribute more

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:07.899
by being neutral, knowing we are weak and
even less interested.

00:16:07.899 --> 00:16:08.899
But that is a joke.

00:16:08.899 --> 00:16:09.980
It's just how it works.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:11.470
It will go to bigger countries.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:22.699
Yes, Namibia is respected in Africa, maybe
because we have been in exile for too long,

00:16:22.699 --> 00:16:27.569
working with people they know already, U.N.
Some would say we-Kramer (sp) will know that

00:16:27.569 --> 00:16:28.990
we-(inaudible)-the United Nations.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:34.019
We were having a council for Namibia, working
with others.

00:16:34.019 --> 00:16:37.720
We are definitely respected, and we are also
resourced.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:43.379
Investors don't come these days because of
good governance per se; they also come for

00:16:43.379 --> 00:16:45.170
resources.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:52.329
So if you have resources like uranium, the
things that people are looking for-right now

00:16:52.329 --> 00:16:58.829
I will tell you, after my inauguration the
invitation list to me is unique that I'm invited

00:16:58.829 --> 00:17:04.580
to pay a state visit will start from China,
Russia, France.

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:08.250
Those are all uranium-connected countries.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:13.920
So these days everybody pays attention to
us because of resources we have.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:20.350
And also in AU we have standing.

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:23.800
Our president-(inaudible)-in formation of
OAU.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:28.420
So we are actually known in Africa.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:30.860
We know African leaders.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:38.620
And I was asked, again first, to go to Brazzaville
for inauguration of President Nguesso, and

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:42.550
you could see that I was the only English-speaking
person.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:49.000
But that opportunity is there and people are
asking to consult.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:51.070
So we are-we are recognized.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.460
But how do we do that?

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:54.910
Example of good governance.

00:17:54.910 --> 00:18:01.070
Good governance-effective governance, I call
it-and service delivery, first leading by

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.850
example, a (declaration ?) was done with PriceWaterhouse.

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:12.620
Some people came from here and they prepared
a paper for me that I could release: Namibia

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:21.540
must be a center for ethics, and you must
now invite the leaders from Africa to have-(inaudible).

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.890
"Hey, don't go there."

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:27.170
Africa, there is respect for elders and so
on.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:31.520
How do I-first, I am so new as a president.

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.390
And many people think I've very young, but
I'm older than many of them, by the way, some

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:39.300
of the presidents, but they all think that
I'm younger than them, and I take it that

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:40.770
way.

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:45.560
And how do I invite, as a young person, older
people to come and lecture them?

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:47.390
That's not how we do it.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:52.070
But if you live by example, example is like
keeping time.

00:18:52.070 --> 00:18:53.420
Small things.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:57.630
When I was appointed to the Cabinet I had
to ask CVs from ministers.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:03.890
They first thought I was trying to interfere
in their business.

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:04.890
There are resistance.

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:08.870
The CVs, then trying to match the skills with
the position they're going to hold.

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:14.060
Then, after that, appointed them contract,
management-I mean, the performance contracts.

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:20.670
Again, these are new things, then to say why
is this man trying to dictate to us, and so

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:21.670
on?

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:25.160
And then I go in and I'm telling them it's
for I have to deliver now.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.420
Our task is to deliver.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:33.880
Others were setting up the stage, the foundation
for peace, unity, democracy.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:37.750
But democracy, now, people don't eat democracy.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:43.250
They want food, shelter, so therefore you
must to enhance all of us so that can work

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:44.250
in deliverance.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:45.250
Time-keeping.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:48.880
If my Cabinet meeting is to start 9:00, it
must start 9:00.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:54.670
The first time ministers will come in maybe
late, they saw me and the second time they

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:55.670
are there 9:00 sharp.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:59.730
The meeting starts; 1:00 we go out, and then
to go and work.

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:01.540
And then involving the people.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:06.520
Town hall meetings concept I copied from you
people.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:08.470
And I went to all 14 regions.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:13.960
Many have ordinary people sitting there for
four or five hours, people talking, talking,

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.950
and then also other business people.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.180
And so I engaged them-engaged people.

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:20.180
But I'm not alone-(inaudible)-them.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:25.490
They all talk, because if I fail to explain
(the cold economics to them ?), and then come

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:29.150
up with the plan that we are calling Harambee
plan.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:31.240
Now, that, it went through.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:35.720
Africa-first Kenyans liked it because we are
using their Swahili word.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:38.070
East Africans say, oh, that's very good.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:41.100
And then that then is now going around.

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:46.550
So that kind of things are small things, but
you recognize-and you must be humble also.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:50.940
We are very respectful to elders, to senior
people.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:55.120
As I said, African leaders, I behave like
I am the youngest.

00:20:55.120 --> 00:21:01.120
But almost, except for a few, most of them
I'm older than them.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.220
But they were longer in their office; therefore
they are senior to me.

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:08.000
So, yes, Namibia will still make headway.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.070
We are just starting.

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:12.600
GAVIN: Indeed.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.820
It's easy to forget how young the country
is.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.440
And it's also young demographically, like
so much of Africa.

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:24.030
Over half the population I think, of Namibia,
is under the age of 25.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:30.630
And so then most Namibians were born after
independence.

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:34.920
And as you say, what they want is-
GEINGOB: We call them "born-frees."

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:35.920
(Laughter.)

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:42.360
GAVIN: Indeed, the born-frees-just as in South
Africa, the born-frees.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:50.330
And the challenge of meeting the demands of
the born-frees, right, who are exposed to

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:59.890
the same images of wealth and prosperity on
social media that any young person here is,

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:10.950
have the same aspirations for their future,
brings me back to this question about poverty

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.410
alleviation and creating new economic opportunity.

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:16.860
And I guess a couple parts to my question.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.090
First, do those young people-do they participate
in these town halls?

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:23.120
Is that how you hear from them?

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:24.610
Is that the mechanism?

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:30.110
Or are they finding new means of political
expression?

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:35.410
And then, you know, when you're looking to
solve the big problem-the job creation imperative,

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:46.830
the economic future of the country, given
that foreign assistance is largely not available

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:52.920
because of Namibia's status, trade and investment-the
theory of the case, right, is that the private

00:22:52.920 --> 00:23:00.710
sector should fill that gap; trade and investment
should fill that gap.

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:02.560
Where is that happening and where is it not?

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:09.680
I know there's significant Chinese investment,
certainly in Namibia-I believe more than 40

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:17.170
Chinese firms operating, bringing in-according
to an article I recently read-some 4.6 billion

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:20.740
U.S. dollars in revenue annually.

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:30.000
Are you seeing the same kind of aggressive
investment strategy from other economic powers?

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.120
GEINGOB: Yes, they should.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:43.550
We have a slogan that I coined: Namibia is
a child of international solidarity, midwifed

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:50.980
by United Nations, so friend to all, enemy
to none.

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:55.200
That's the cornerstone of our foreign policy.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:24:04.430
And therefore, when we are saying, now, government
must look towards those who are needy and

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:13.340
provide conducive environment for those who
are able to be on their own, using the conducive

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:18.550
investment climate we are providing, an invitation
is open to everybody.

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:24.480
Canadians are also there in a big way with
that kind of-mention the Chinese with the

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:27.690
mining sector, uranium, and also copper.

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:34.950
I just launched the smelter acid plant in
Tsumeb two weeks ago.

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:37.220
The Canadians are there.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:43.950
I had a very good, successful visit by congressional
delegation led by Senator Flake.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:52.080
We are going to receive a business delegation
in May, Ambassador-in May, yeah.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.850
And it's open to everybody.

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:58.440
Chinese factor is a factor-reality in the
world.

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.010
You know, I'm a Waldorf Astoria person.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:07.060
I was put there the first time by Tempelsman
and them, and I said, why are they putting

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:08.210
me in that place?

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:13.200
I discovered it is a cheaper one compared
to where I was staying, in the Continental

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:14.400
Hotel-far, far cheaper.

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:16.810
So we are scared of Waldorf.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:23.970
So can you imagine a landmark like Waldorf
Astoria to have been bought by Chinese?

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:25.990
Here in New York City.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:28.120
They are everywhere, the Chinese.

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:30.870
It's a big population.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:39.900
And Namibia of course has to be careful, because
I was in Lusaka when the TAZARA road was being

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:40.900
built.

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:43.140
We never saw Chinese socializing.

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:48.270
But of course we have to also give credit
that the Chinese have opened up even China.

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:54.220
But in Namibia we are already seeing-what
do you call them-(inaudible)?

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.980
You see the children coming.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:03.770
That's very serious, because if you have a
child, you can bring the family to see the

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:04.770
children.

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:05.770
It's family.

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:08.490
So, being a small population, you have to
be careful.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:13.410
One day you will wake up only 1 million, 2
million.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:14.410
(Chuckles.)

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:20.130
So it's a-it's a reality, but they are saying
they have opened up to the world.

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:22.480
They are in America.

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.240
They are everywhere.

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:31.400
But we always say we had a very difficult
struggle to free ourselves.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:36.690
Not supported some of the American government,
as I said the other day, supported by those

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:38.960
former communist countries.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:45.420
We fought to free our country, and I don't
think any of them are going to take over,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:50.200
will exchange that freedom to be recognized
by any other person.

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:52.100
We tell Chinese that.

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:59.050
And I copy that statement that I got from
them when I went to China as a minister of

00:26:59.050 --> 00:27:04.330
trade, because there was nearly a fistfight
in Namibia because they were bringing ordinary

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:05.330
workers.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:06.540
The unemployment is so high.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.030
They were not obeying our laws, the labor
laws.

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:13.420
So I said, before you are going to fight,
let me go and tell them what is happening

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:15.560
and what could happen very soon.

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:23.280
So I went there and then they told me-you
know, and I saw sort of Americans there and

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.860
I said, oh, comrades, what's happening?

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:28.010
Americans are taking over here.

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:30.350
They said they have opened up 30 years ago.

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:33.880
They are privatizing and they are open.

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:40.780
But-that "but" is very important-whoever is
here comes on our own terms.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:44.190
So I keep on telling them, don't forget what
you are telling us.

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:50.920
When you come to Namibia you must also come
on our own terms, and great win-win situation.

00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:54.900
And I will not deny that we are happy with
some of the investments.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:59.710
First, we don't eat uranium.

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:02.860
We don't even know-Namibians don't know what
is uranium.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:06.770
Diamonds, some ladies are now wearing it but
it's not something we eat.

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:11.810
But somebody must need it who add economic
value to it.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:15.070
And China wants to get uranium for whatever
reason.

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:18.700
I think that I don't even know-Namibians want
to eat.

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:26.790
And they come and build a company, a mine
in a desert, build a highway there; 2,000

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:32.080
people who were in the streets are housed,
with running water and lights.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:34.950
I said, that's good.

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:36.390
That's investment.

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:43.550
However-however, how do we benefit from that
to a great win-win situation-always quoting

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:44.550
them.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:52.280
So firstly we agreed your laws must apply,
because I told them, if you don't obey our

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:55.510
laws, our labor laws-overtime must be paid.

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:59.320
You cannot bring wheelbarrow pushers because
of already high unemployment.

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:05.590
Therefore, bring only experts, but not to
bring who do the ordinary work, and not to

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:11.080
compete with retail business, with Namibians
that are involved-small people.

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:12.760
So we did all those things.

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:21.420
So there is no way we, as a land of the brave-we
are not just called "land of the brave" for

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:22.420
a joke.

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:23.910
We fought South Africa, you know?

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:30.020
Even if you compare us with ANC and so forth,
we are fighting South African army.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.201
So we don't believe-and are a very proud people.

00:29:32.201 --> 00:29:38.260
We are saying "the land of the brave" very
proud and very maybe (it's base pride ?). But

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:44.040
we are not going to exchange our freedom with
anybody else coming to take it.

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:49.290
We are very strict and we are-we respect them,
they respect us, to tell the truth.

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:53.390
I met the president of China already two times.

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:59.260
So respect is there, but we've put our case,
where we stand.

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:01.220
I want to cooperate.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.050
And we see there is respect.

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:04.730
There is serious respect.

00:30:04.730 --> 00:30:09.940
And we Africans now believe in that too, not
actually we're taken as small boys who don't

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:11.570
know anything, and so on.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:16.780
I think we want to matter-as we are saying
on Namibia's role, we want to matter in world

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:17.780
affairs.

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:23.210
And if Chinese can build us up, if the Russians
are now coming back to compete with you and

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:27.640
they respect me, if President Obama respects
that, why not?

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:31.610
We're are actors in this world, small or not.

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:32.930
GAVIN: Clearly, clearly.

00:30:32.930 --> 00:30:40.070
And the climate change element of your agenda
here alone sort of is symbolic of that, right?

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:45.210
You're here, and you're here in person to
underscore the importance of this not just

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.070
for Namibia but for the world.

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:54.490
Well, at this point I'd like to invite members
to join the conversation.

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:58.120
Please remember that this meeting is on the
record.

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:03.200
And if you'd like to ask a question just go
ahead and turn your name card to vertical

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:04.520
and I'll do my best.

00:31:04.520 --> 00:31:10.460
I might not be able to see your name because
of the angles, but we'll soldier on.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:13.950
And when you're selected, please do speak
into a microphone.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:14.950
State your name and affiliation.

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:19.870
And if I could please just ask everyone to
keep their question concise.

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:24.320
Even at the Council it's rare to get such
a treat to have an opportunity to chat with

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:31.810
a head of state, and so we don't want to lose
time delivering a lot of our own remarks.

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:36.420
So with that, why don't we start with Maurice
Tempelsman?

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:42.150
Q: Thank you, Michelle.

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:52.580
Coming back to the Harambee plan, which is
a very well-thought-through and comprehensive

00:31:52.580 --> 00:32:02.470
roadmap, and then moving to what you indicated
as being a new president but you bring to

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:12.500
this position a rare and unusual combination
of qualities, the first one obviously a political

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:18.700
knowledge and sensitivity but you're also
blessed with the capacity or reputation of

00:32:18.700 --> 00:32:22.360
being a terrific administrator.

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:31.160
And that brings to mind a question or point,
lens through which a former president of a

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:36.810
neighboring country-where Ambassador Gavin
used to stay-Festus Mogae, and he was fond

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:43.660
of saying: If everything is a priority, then
nothing is a priority.

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:51.410
Looking at it through that lens, what are
your priorities in terms of execution of your

00:32:51.410 --> 00:32:53.200
Harambee plan?

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:56.680
GEINGOB: Well, as I said-thank you, Maurice.

00:32:56.680 --> 00:32:59.340
We've come a long way.

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:05.610
Yes, I sort of learned through that long experience
to be a team player.

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:09.060
That's a strong-you know, so I have team.

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:15.870
John, can you-or first I've decided on Minister
Tom Alweendo.

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:19.790
I have the deputy prime minister.

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:23.810
So they all must play a role.

00:33:23.810 --> 00:33:32.480
But let's start, since you are our planning
minister and economic minister.

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.990
ALWEENDO: Thank you, Comrade President.

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:37.400
Thank you, Comrade President.

00:33:37.400 --> 00:33:43.400
Maybe to just start off with what we have-start
off by saying, you know, the time has come

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:47.980
for, I think, Namibia to do some extraordinary
things.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:53.570
Twenty-six years, people are now demanding
different things, not more like-you know,

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:56.750
yes, we have the peace but now we need to
develop prosperity.

00:33:56.750 --> 00:34:02.650
But that can only happen if we are going to
do certain things, and hence the Harambee

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:08.450
plan, which has got, you know, pillars, those
seven pillars which we realize that we need

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:09.450
to do.

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:11.470
You know, we talk about economic transformation.

00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:14.609
We talk about governance and deliveries.

00:34:14.609 --> 00:34:16.280
We talk about infrastructure development.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:23.970
Those are all important pillars which we need
to embrace, invest in, for us to be able then

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:28.029
to develop the prosperity which we need.

00:34:28.029 --> 00:34:34.899
But that can also only happen with the assistance
of our partners.

00:34:34.899 --> 00:34:38.639
I mean, earlier on we were talking about,
you know, us being an upper-middle-income

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:44.369
country and therefore not getting the assistance
we-other people are getting.

00:34:44.369 --> 00:34:48.290
But there is also something I think we can
still get from our partners.

00:34:48.290 --> 00:34:52.869
We talk about, for example, in the Harambee,
the economic transformation.

00:34:52.869 --> 00:34:57.069
That transformation of the economic can take
place only if we industrialize.

00:34:57.069 --> 00:35:00.240
And we have agreed that we need to industrialize.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:05.809
And Namibia-and not only Namibia, also the
whole continent, that transformation through

00:35:05.809 --> 00:35:11.599
industrialization can only also happen if
we start by adding value to the commodities

00:35:11.599 --> 00:35:16.480
which you all export from the continent.

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:21.009
There we can get some assistance from our
partners, and that assistance in terms of

00:35:21.009 --> 00:35:24.619
how we trade-how we've been trading all this
time.

00:35:24.619 --> 00:35:32.500
The value addition we are talking about on
the commodities which we export in raw forms,

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:38.170
we need to engage the private sector because
it's the private sector naturally that has

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:41.640
been the exporters of those raw materials.

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:47.069
And by and large, those private sectors are
not always African private sectors.

00:35:47.069 --> 00:35:50.970
It's private sectors usually from developed
nations.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:56.140
And therefore, we would like to have our partners
from those countries to agree with us, to

00:35:56.140 --> 00:36:04.070
say their multinational companies need to
agree to add values to our raw materials.

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:09.019
And that's, I think, where I think we can
really get that partnership, that support

00:36:09.019 --> 00:36:15.200
from the likes of you to encourage those multinationals
to play the same role which we want to play

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:23.410
by creating prosperity through industrializations
and through beneficiation of our commodities.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:26.170
That is, I think, what I can say about the
Harambee.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:30.950
But as I say, there are not only the transformation
but there are other pillars like, you know,

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:37.559
as I say, deliveries, governance, because
without governance-like we talked about earlier

00:36:37.559 --> 00:36:43.099
on, people do start believing that, yes, these
are all possible things we can all do.

00:36:43.099 --> 00:36:47.769
That is very, very important to have that
mindset that says, yes, we can achieve all

00:36:47.769 --> 00:36:51.780
these big things which we have put ourselves
to deliver.

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:54.290
GEINGOB: And-(inaudible)-please, sir.

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:58.480
STEYTLER: Thank you, Comrade President.

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:06.690
Just to the question of Mr. Tempelsman, in
terms of priorities the plan is built around

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:08.509
four pillars.

00:37:08.509 --> 00:37:15.289
But if I can single out two priorities that
His Excellency has singled out, number one

00:37:15.289 --> 00:37:19.849
would be to fight corruption.

00:37:19.849 --> 00:37:23.190
We have declared full-out war on corruption.

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:29.039
And therefore, in terms of the first pillar,
Maurice, we are targeting to become the most

00:37:29.039 --> 00:37:35.700
accountable country on the African continent,
as measured by the Mo Ibrahim Index.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:42.700
We are also planning to retain our space as
the country that's rated as the freest in

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:49.390
terms of price freedom, as measured by Reporters
Without Borders.

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:53.250
We also want to improve service delivery.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:38:00.511
And one priority for us is also to become
the most competitive economy in Africa, not

00:38:00.511 --> 00:38:01.839
just in SADC.

00:38:01.839 --> 00:38:07.431
We are currently number five but we want to,
within a period of four years, say that we

00:38:07.431 --> 00:38:08.839
are most competitive.

00:38:08.839 --> 00:38:13.880
And for us to do that we are looking really
at a business environment.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:19.640
We would like to streamline business processes,
making it easier for a business-the private

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:23.960
sector to operate, because the private sector
is the ultimate creators of wealth.

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:32.210
Then another key, key priority for the government
will be the declaration on poverty, the war

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:33.210
on poverty.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:40.579
Now, this is a war that will have to be won
over many years, but under the Harambee period

00:38:40.579 --> 00:38:46.450
we start with hunger poverty and we say that
no Namibians should die because of hunger.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:58.270
Then there are also a key priority on vocational,
technical education, and also some priorities

00:38:58.270 --> 00:39:02.009
in the area of infrastructure development.

00:39:02.009 --> 00:39:09.200
So these are some of the priorities, Maurice,
that I can highlight from the plan.

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:10.200
Thank you.

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:11.230
GAVIN: Thank you all so much.

00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:12.950
Gail Gerhart?

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:16.390
Q: Is this on?

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:29.670
GAVIN: The red light should come on.

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:30.670
There we are.

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:32.079
Oh, sorry.

00:39:32.079 --> 00:39:41.220
Q: Could you explain to us the current relationship
of Namibia with the government of North Korea?

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:49.549
This has been publicized in the press now
and again, and it's been pointed out that

00:39:49.549 --> 00:39:56.369
Namibia's in violation of the U.N. agreements
about cooperation with North Korea.

00:39:56.369 --> 00:40:00.609
Can you give us an explanation of your current
position on that?

00:40:00.609 --> 00:40:04.210
GEINGOB: Yes, thank you.

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:18.099
As I said, we used to fight apartheid regime
after we exhausted all peaceful means.

00:40:18.099 --> 00:40:21.329
I came here as a young boy in '64.

00:40:21.329 --> 00:40:30.289
And then I was made a petitioner at the U.N.,
begging everybody to help us, to free us peacefully.

00:40:30.289 --> 00:40:39.140
The United States has relations with South
Africa, Israel, Portugal, those who were oppressing

00:40:39.140 --> 00:40:43.210
our people at that time-bad regimes to me.

00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:46.470
But because of national interest, you had
relations.

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:54.119
We're doing resolutions calling for us to
get our independence and human rights.

00:40:54.119 --> 00:40:57.640
That's a national interest serving American
interests.

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:02.220
So after independence these countries continued
to have relations with us.

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:09.839
They helped us during the struggle, and therefore
we invite them to come and also join us as

00:41:09.839 --> 00:41:15.319
we are doing even today, to be involved with
us to develop the country like any other business

00:41:15.319 --> 00:41:16.319
asset.

00:41:16.319 --> 00:41:23.850
Koreans came a long time ago to build the
statehouse that we are living in before the

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:28.390
sanctions, and that relationship was there.

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:36.200
And then we also had to build some structures
like the Heroes' Acre we call it, where people-like

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:41.890
you have a-what do you say, a grave you have
in Washington-Arlington.

00:41:41.890 --> 00:41:43.680
GAVIN: Arlington National Cemetery.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:45.019
GEINGOB: Arlington, yes.

00:41:45.019 --> 00:41:51.310
So you have people who have sacrificed their
lives during the war and they must be buried

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:53.099
properly, or recognized.

00:41:53.099 --> 00:41:54.099
We have that.

00:41:54.099 --> 00:41:55.099
We have-(inaudible).

00:41:55.099 --> 00:41:56.099
They built that.

00:41:56.099 --> 00:42:00.630
So Koreans were there at that time, before
sanctions came.

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:03.760
Plus also some monuments.

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:11.470
If you go to Namibia, you will only see any
structure standing there is German structure,

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:12.779
nothing African.

00:42:12.779 --> 00:42:17.039
But, remember, we had mud huts, so therefore
they cannot stand as structures.

00:42:17.039 --> 00:42:19.390
So you only have these German structure.

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:23.799
We're bringing our own legacy also as people.

00:42:23.799 --> 00:42:30.070
And don't say it's Chinese or Korean buildings.

00:42:30.070 --> 00:42:36.700
It is our-we are the ones who, as I say, build
this and this.

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:40.600
Like, you got your famous Statue of Liberty;
it's a gift from France.

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:42.730
It's not you, but it's France.

00:42:42.730 --> 00:42:49.210
So it's yours, so that's how we have those
structures, since it's been built by Koreans.

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:56.369
When the sanctions came in, we had to sit
down and see, where are we in conflict.

00:42:56.369 --> 00:43:02.579
Nothing is retroactive, the way I was trained
by your universities that were mentioned.

00:43:02.579 --> 00:43:07.950
The law that was not there when crime was
committed is not applicable to that situation.

00:43:07.950 --> 00:43:15.839
So some of the things that build in our statehouse
was done before the sanctions came in.

00:43:15.839 --> 00:43:21.069
Only sanctions now comprehensive was a recent
one of about-is 2006?

00:43:21.069 --> 00:43:22.259
What is it?

00:43:22.259 --> 00:43:23.259
MR./MS.

00:43:23.259 --> 00:43:24.259
: (Off mic.)

00:43:24.259 --> 00:43:25.259
GEINGOB: Yeah.

00:43:25.259 --> 00:43:30.289
That is the only sanctions that we may be
in violation of.

00:43:30.289 --> 00:43:35.079
We talk to the American ambassador, explain
to him, even before I came.

00:43:35.079 --> 00:43:40.210
We made appointment with the chairman of the-of
the Sanctions Committee.

00:43:40.210 --> 00:43:45.499
By the way, we should also be careful: Is
it the American sanctions or United Nations

00:43:45.499 --> 00:43:46.499
sanctions?

00:43:46.499 --> 00:43:48.460
Whose sanction is it?

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:50.180
Q: The United Nations sanctions.

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:51.180
GEINGOB: Oh, United Nations sanction.

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:54.480
So we are accountable to the United Nations,
not to Americans, OK?

00:43:54.480 --> 00:43:55.480
(Laughs.)

00:43:55.480 --> 00:43:57.960
So we are going to see that committee to explain
to them.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:00.339
We are just going to report.

00:44:00.339 --> 00:44:04.010
And it's not a question that we are committing
a crime.

00:44:04.010 --> 00:44:09.930
These things are new, whereby they are testing
their nuclear weapons.

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:11.599
Even Chinese have complained.

00:44:11.599 --> 00:44:14.400
So, now, that's a new development.

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:20.339
But what they were doing there was not illegal
or violating any sanctions.

00:44:20.339 --> 00:44:23.059
But comprehensive sanctions, it's just about
two months out.

00:44:23.059 --> 00:44:28.799
And that's what we are going to discuss with
them, how-in fact, we already comply when

00:44:28.799 --> 00:44:38.309
the one company was listed, or two people
who are listed as maybe cover-undercover agents

00:44:38.309 --> 00:44:41.740
for the regime, we sent them away.

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:47.109
So we complied with the sanctions.

00:44:47.109 --> 00:44:53.619
But the one which is now comprehensive is
raising things like the defense headquarters,

00:44:53.619 --> 00:44:56.980
which was-it started long time ago.

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:59.670
So we are going to discuss that with United
Nations.

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:03.109
But I'm glad to inform Americans that's what
we are doing.

00:45:03.109 --> 00:45:06.380
GAVIN: Well, thank you very much, Mr. President.

00:45:06.380 --> 00:45:11.980
And so, just to clarify, what I understand
you to be saying is that it is your position

00:45:11.980 --> 00:45:20.250
that Namibia is accountable for adhering to
United-U.N. agreed-upon sanctions, and this

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:22.460
is-this is what the discussion is about.

00:45:22.460 --> 00:45:27.200
GEINGOB: Yeah, I told the American ambassador
we are a United Nations creation.

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:32.440
There is no (way we consent in a way ?) of
violating United Nations resolution.

00:45:32.440 --> 00:45:34.400
GAVIN: Thank you.

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:35.700
Thank you.

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:42.089
I'm sorry, it's a-it's my age, and I cannot
make-but the woman right in front of me, yes.

00:45:42.089 --> 00:45:43.089
Thank you so very much.

00:45:43.089 --> 00:45:46.450
I apologize for that.

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:50.279
Q: (Off mic.)

00:45:50.279 --> 00:45:51.559
Oh.

00:45:51.559 --> 00:45:53.410
(Comes on mic.)

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:54.410
Mojibau Aldoulou (ph) from Keokome (ph).

00:45:54.410 --> 00:46:06.599
My question is about the born-free generation,
their aspirations, because how similar and

00:46:06.599 --> 00:46:12.259
how different are those aspirations from that
of the older generation?

00:46:12.259 --> 00:46:20.089
And especially in terms of education, from
what I gather, you know, there is a lot of

00:46:20.089 --> 00:46:28.450
interest on the part of the government to
focus on vocational and technical education.

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:36.799
How does this jell with what the youth want
and their aspirations?

00:46:36.799 --> 00:46:40.309
GEINGOB: Very good question.

00:46:40.309 --> 00:46:45.569
Firstly, there is generational gap, I think.

00:46:45.569 --> 00:46:53.970
We are, who are old-timers, when I'm campaigning,
I can go and tell the old people we fought

00:46:53.970 --> 00:46:59.880
for freedom, you know how bad-how bad it was,
and they get-they connect.

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:07.060
Youth-my daughter, my kids-don't know; so
what, they may say: I'm unemployed, I want

00:47:07.060 --> 00:47:08.799
education.

00:47:08.799 --> 00:47:13.559
The information age people, they are on these
things.

00:47:13.559 --> 00:47:17.950
They don't care about-we were talking about
how bad-how bad it was.

00:47:17.950 --> 00:47:18.950
That's not their concern.

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:24.769
They are unemployed, they want education,
they want high-tech.

00:47:24.769 --> 00:47:26.109
That's their concern.

00:47:26.109 --> 00:47:32.779
So you will be wasting your time if you go
and talk to them about how we struggled against

00:47:32.779 --> 00:47:34.299
South Africa.

00:47:34.299 --> 00:47:36.400
You lose them.

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:41.962
So even vocational education, which looks
like you are taking them back to work with

00:47:41.962 --> 00:47:44.550
hands, you have to be careful about that.

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:51.619
You have to actually talk to them, as I did
when I was director of U.N. Institute for

00:47:51.619 --> 00:47:54.400
Namibia in Lusaka.

00:47:54.400 --> 00:48:02.739
We had in our organization training-agriculture,
management-and we allowed them to register,

00:48:02.739 --> 00:48:08.400
and there was zero interest in agriculture
because they thought agriculture was for blacks

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:11.819
who are oppressed, dirty your hands, and so
on.

00:48:11.819 --> 00:48:14.650
So they didn't register voluntarily.

00:48:14.650 --> 00:48:19.720
So I had to have talks with them to say, comrades,
you see, we're going to serve the masses.

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:21.400
Yes, yes, yes.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:24.250
What's the population of Windhoek and other
towns I mentioned?

00:48:24.250 --> 00:48:31.270
It came to about half a million, and Namibia
is about one-point-something million.

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:34.130
Now, where are the masses?

00:48:34.130 --> 00:48:36.470
Oh, they're in the-in the rural areas.

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:39.940
Oh, comrades, therefore, agriculture is where
they are.

00:48:39.940 --> 00:48:41.079
That's how you talk to them.

00:48:41.079 --> 00:48:44.809
And that thing became oversubscribed, even
women.

00:48:44.809 --> 00:48:46.849
So vocational training is their thing.

00:48:46.849 --> 00:48:51.920
I make a joke when I'm talking: Ph.D.s do
not run the countries.

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:58.490
When we are sitting like this-concrete example:
if that air conditioner is out, with my Ph.D.,

00:48:58.490 --> 00:49:00.430
I cannot fix it.

00:49:00.430 --> 00:49:02.460
So it's people-and Germany was built by artisans.

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:07.900
We have German-we have a lot of Germans out
there, so I have to link to that.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:11.069
Germans use their hands, and look at where
Germany is.

00:49:11.069 --> 00:49:13.690
That's a talk you must give them.

00:49:13.690 --> 00:49:15.650
Otherwise, you lose them.

00:49:15.650 --> 00:49:19.109
And they are also getting their social-status
degrees; they cannot get jobs.

00:49:19.109 --> 00:49:21.759
So they are educated, but on the street.

00:49:21.759 --> 00:49:26.170
And that's what I was told about Boko Haram,
huh, that President Obama or someone was telling

00:49:26.170 --> 00:49:28.960
me that-how it started.

00:49:28.960 --> 00:49:38.990
The guy who was leader was having education,
Western education, degree, but he was unemployed.

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:44.140
So he says-other guys came and said, you are
educated in the Western world, you have education,

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:46.390
why are you unemployed?

00:49:46.390 --> 00:49:49.599
That's how they say destroy Western education.

00:49:49.599 --> 00:49:53.910
So same may be true of our young people: they
have degrees, but they are unemployed.

00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:55.400
So it's a delicate thing.

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:58.800
They are a majority of voters, too, now, very
soon.

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:00.859
And they are demanding.

00:50:00.859 --> 00:50:06.660
They are also acting in Namibia, deliberately
trying to-what do you call their muscles,

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:09.660
to show that they have power.

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:17.089
They just went and grabbed the land while
I was still campaigning-organized and grabbed

00:50:17.089 --> 00:50:25.960
the land, and set a deadline of 31st of July
the economy was going to go up in flames.

00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:28.280
And the party was divided.

00:50:28.280 --> 00:50:31.250
I just became the leader of the party.

00:50:31.250 --> 00:50:34.040
And people said, don't talk to them.

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:41.900
And first meeting of the-(inaudible)-I had
to expel them from the party, but as a president

00:50:41.900 --> 00:50:43.799
I must talk to them.

00:50:43.799 --> 00:50:52.910
So I held a meeting with them on the 24th
of July last year, six hours talks, allowing

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:56.400
them to make their case and sitting with them
for six hours.

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:57.549
That's how we preserved.

00:50:57.549 --> 00:51:02.440
Maybe there could have been a-you could have
read in the papers Namibia is up in flames.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:06.140
Small things happen like that, and therefore
talk, engage the people.

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:09.750
I told them, look, in democracy, you don't
have to go to war.

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:13.529
Bring up things here; we can talk.

00:51:13.529 --> 00:51:16.740
So the good thing about democracy is a dialogue.

00:51:16.740 --> 00:51:21.569
Diplomats are telling us, can't just go to
war when diplomacy fails.

00:51:21.569 --> 00:51:27.420
So therefore, if you don't talk to young people,
and let them win also.

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:35.210
After we had educated them, they had a deadline
of 31st of July.

00:51:35.210 --> 00:51:37.720
So I said, we are going to service the land.

00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:42.440
After we talked, I said there should be no
loser.

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:49.329
So let's say we're going to service the land
from 27th of July up to 5th of August, so

00:51:49.329 --> 00:51:50.759
you don't lose.

00:51:50.759 --> 00:51:55.519
Because if we don't do it on your date, I
won.

00:51:55.519 --> 00:52:00.710
So let's say that I just arrange, therefore
you also won-your day is included.

00:52:00.710 --> 00:52:02.630
Great, a winning situation.

00:52:02.630 --> 00:52:03.630
But listen to them.

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:05.259
They are very nasty.

00:52:05.259 --> 00:52:08.559
I am also on the Facebook and social media.

00:52:08.559 --> 00:52:10.210
I just engage them.

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:11.960
That's their dialogue, doing things.

00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:17.670
And they appreciate, because you are encountering
them there, answering things, explaining.

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:25.170
And many of them, I may get five nasty tweets,
but about 95 percent will support me because

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:26.500
I explain the situation.

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:28.559
Engage them.

00:52:28.559 --> 00:52:29.559
Engage them at their level.

00:52:29.559 --> 00:52:31.400
That's the social media.

00:52:31.400 --> 00:52:38.740
GAVIN: That's a little free political advice
for all of us.

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:40.590
Mr. Nakayama, yes.

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:44.290
Q: President, my is Nakayama, Citigroup.

00:52:44.290 --> 00:52:51.089
I have a question about wisdom two countries
can share, U.S. and Namibia.

00:52:51.089 --> 00:52:52.450
Two countries have a challenge.

00:52:52.450 --> 00:52:57.039
Your country is very heterogeneous, like United
States.

00:52:57.039 --> 00:53:00.039
Under your strong leadership, the country
has united.

00:53:00.039 --> 00:53:04.319
What United States can learn from you or your
country about wisdom?

00:53:04.319 --> 00:53:12.470
And, second, what United States should do
to create long-term, strong, sustainable relationship

00:53:12.470 --> 00:53:17.609
with Namibia to become most important partner
to your country?

00:53:17.609 --> 00:53:27.089
GEINGOB: Firstly, I must tell you-(laughs)-United
States taught me to how to put different people

00:53:27.089 --> 00:53:28.089
together.

00:53:28.089 --> 00:53:32.051
As I said, at the time of '60s, that's the
time of black power.

00:53:32.051 --> 00:53:36.119
That's the time of burn, baby, burn.

00:53:36.119 --> 00:53:47.960
And I also learn in this country that being-having
an open society, and during the Vietnam War

00:53:47.960 --> 00:53:58.819
I used to see professors from Ivy League schools-Princeton-Richard
Falk was from Princeton, and I am from Columbia-debating.

00:53:58.819 --> 00:54:03.640
Coming from South Africa-I mean, Namibia,
which isn't allowing those things, I said,

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:09.329
hey, these guys are now debating, disagreeing,
and so on.

00:54:09.329 --> 00:54:10.329
And then you take the Supreme Court.

00:54:10.329 --> 00:54:16.390
Supreme Court are the one-is the one which
has helped blacks, through the rulings of

00:54:16.390 --> 00:54:17.849
the Court, to create the harmony.

00:54:17.849 --> 00:54:19.910
So you shouldn't play down your role.

00:54:19.910 --> 00:54:24.599
We are also copying from what you have achieved.

00:54:24.599 --> 00:54:30.180
And also, what I have learned here is that
an open admission, open discussion-a problem

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:35.920
we have in Namibia and South Africa is there
are some white people who don't want to think

00:54:35.920 --> 00:54:37.099
of the past.

00:54:37.099 --> 00:54:43.440
They think Namibia started to be a country
from 21st of March, 1990.

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:49.849
So if you talk about the past, they think
we are going back to the past, the ugly history.

00:54:49.849 --> 00:54:53.160
But here, what makes (the movement ?) is everything
is discussed.

00:54:53.160 --> 00:54:56.470
It looks ugly, maybe; people look as they
are fighting.

00:54:56.470 --> 00:54:58.220
But you solve it that way.

00:54:58.220 --> 00:55:02.890
So I cannot teach you anything in this country.

00:55:02.890 --> 00:55:04.759
You taught us.

00:55:04.759 --> 00:55:09.430
But you also moved farther.

00:55:09.430 --> 00:55:14.660
(You jumped the ?)-(inaudible)-by having a
black president.

00:55:14.660 --> 00:55:23.650
I mean, that is-you might play it down, but
as Africa got independence in 1957, Kwame

00:55:23.650 --> 00:55:27.789
Nkrumah became the first president-black president,
so to say.

00:55:27.789 --> 00:55:33.380
And black Americans were encouraged, looking
up to Africa, that Pan-Africanism started-d'Ivoire,

00:55:33.380 --> 00:55:40.040
went to Ghana, and so on, started from there.

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:47.119
Now, President Obama being the president of
the most powerful country also help many Africans

00:55:47.119 --> 00:55:48.450
that us do proud.

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:59.280
If you go to Asia-some of you know Asia-sometimes
they'll be more racial, in a very unique way.

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:06.549
But when you walk around as a black man and
the American president is also black, it also

00:56:06.549 --> 00:56:07.780
brings something.

00:56:07.780 --> 00:56:10.259
So there's not much I can teach you about
that.

00:56:10.259 --> 00:56:11.559
I think you are doing very well.

00:56:11.559 --> 00:56:12.799
I worked here.

00:56:12.799 --> 00:56:14.369
I-not work, I started here.

00:56:14.369 --> 00:56:17.690
It was during that time more difficult.

00:56:17.690 --> 00:56:21.359
But tolerance, things are happening.

00:56:21.359 --> 00:56:24.579
But, you see, it's not overlooked.

00:56:24.579 --> 00:56:27.950
NACCP-I forgot-
GAVIN: NAACP, mmm hmm.

00:56:27.950 --> 00:56:31.319
GEINGOB: -was helped by white people.

00:56:31.319 --> 00:56:36.700
Lawyers were-white people were-during the
march in the South, white were killed-three

00:56:36.700 --> 00:56:37.700
whites were killed.

00:56:37.700 --> 00:56:41.240
That's how the whole thing started.

00:56:41.240 --> 00:56:42.560
So keep it up.

00:56:42.560 --> 00:56:43.560
Just discuss.

00:56:43.560 --> 00:56:45.200
You are a great country.

00:56:45.200 --> 00:56:48.619
I cannot teach you anything about that because
you are doing well.

00:56:48.619 --> 00:56:49.619
Leaning from you.

00:56:49.619 --> 00:56:54.720
But, of course, Namibia also had colonial
period.

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:59.109
South Africa learned from there, too.

00:56:59.109 --> 00:57:07.329
In the house I'm talking about, where there
is a slogan: no one must feel left out.

00:57:07.329 --> 00:57:15.470
Not no one must be left out; no one must feel
left out, how that person feels.

00:57:15.470 --> 00:57:19.509
So don't give the idea of feeling that they
are left out.

00:57:19.509 --> 00:57:26.249
But only if the president was black, how can
anybody say blacks are left out?

00:57:26.249 --> 00:57:27.249
So keep it up.

00:57:27.249 --> 00:57:32.910
As for Namibia and United States, very good
relations, definitely.

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:40.650
Starting also from mandate system is Woodrow
Wilson, who said there should be no spoils

00:57:40.650 --> 00:57:41.650
of war.

00:57:41.650 --> 00:57:43.869
That's how the mandate system was established.

00:57:43.869 --> 00:57:47.320
And when we were at the International Court
of Justice, the United States was there with

00:57:47.320 --> 00:57:48.320
us.

00:57:48.320 --> 00:57:53.420
We only parted ways when-(inaudible)-how to
go about after the Court failed to deliver.

00:57:53.420 --> 00:57:56.710
Now the U.N. was terminating the mandate of
South Africa.

00:57:56.710 --> 00:57:59.390
That's where we disagreed, on the modalities
how to go about.

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:03.740
So, as I said, Maurice (sp) and them, they
have been with us.

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:05.410
We go a long way.

00:58:05.410 --> 00:58:08.390
We have many friends, American friends that
we have here.

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:09.710
So when we're in trouble, we'll call on you.

00:58:09.710 --> 00:58:10.710
Don't worry.

00:58:10.710 --> 00:58:11.710
We are together.

00:58:11.710 --> 00:58:14.109
GAVIN: This is advice and a pep talk.

00:58:14.109 --> 00:58:15.109
(Laughter.)

00:58:15.109 --> 00:58:16.109
A: Thank you, President.

00:58:16.109 --> 00:58:17.700
GAVIN: Want you here every week.

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:19.839
Thank you so much.

00:58:19.839 --> 00:58:25.829
You have been very specific about the importance
of keeping time, and they are sticklers for

00:58:25.829 --> 00:58:27.109
this at the Council as well.

00:58:27.109 --> 00:58:31.789
So we're supposed to wrap up at seven, so
I'm going to ask Tammy (sp), please, to give

00:58:31.789 --> 00:58:35.819
us the last question, and we'll all be very
concise.

00:58:35.819 --> 00:58:37.559
Q: Thank you.

00:58:37.559 --> 00:58:43.479
Mr. President, in your State of the Nation
address on Harambee, you explained to the

00:58:43.479 --> 00:58:50.539
country the pillars that Mr. Alweendo-Minister
Alweendo discussed, and talked about sub-pillars,

00:58:50.539 --> 00:58:53.200
and much of the speech was the overview.

00:58:53.200 --> 00:58:59.239
But you also gave some examples that brought
the plan alive in terms of its effects on

00:58:59.239 --> 00:59:06.150
ordinary Namibians, such as the recruitment
and training of unemployed youth to administer

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:12.460
food banks in urban areas, and in rural areas
to build toilets and to provide sanitation,

00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:16.869
bringing together-simultaneously addressing
the youth unemployment and some of the issues

00:59:16.869 --> 00:59:20.729
of hunger and need for sanitation and toilets.

00:59:20.729 --> 00:59:27.440
Could you give just a few more examples of
how the Harambee plan in all its overviews

00:59:27.440 --> 00:59:32.349
can come down to the local level and address
the problems of people?

00:59:32.349 --> 00:59:39.229
GEINGOB: Tammy (sp) and Reid (sp) are old
friends-again, people who have-whom I work

00:59:39.229 --> 00:59:40.229
with.

00:59:40.229 --> 00:59:43.220
And they were in Namibia just recently.

00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:50.400
So, yes, Harambee plan is really a detailed
plan.

00:59:50.400 --> 00:59:59.960
The youth-there is a specific thing in engaging
the youth: vocational training; plus, also,

00:59:59.960 --> 01:00:06.650
when it comes to-the food bank idea came-I
was in a church, and I pick up one lady who

01:00:06.650 --> 01:00:11.589
is a daughter of one of the icons of the struggle,
and I was driving.

01:00:11.589 --> 01:00:18.680
As we were driving to an outside location-really
where blacks are leaving, sorry to say-but

01:00:18.680 --> 01:00:22.970
there were kids who were sitting, young boys,
angry, as we were driving.

01:00:22.970 --> 01:00:26.430
And I saw them angry, and then I dropped her.

01:00:26.430 --> 01:00:30.300
She took me around to the houses, looked super
angry.

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:33.839
(Inaudible)-when they're angry, (woman ?) said,
yes.

01:00:33.839 --> 01:00:39.329
Then the idea of the food bank came from there,
that I said, look, we can use-(inaudible)-principle

01:00:39.329 --> 01:00:41.849
of having stage committees.

01:00:41.849 --> 01:00:46.299
Get those boys who are angry and give them
responsibility.

01:00:46.299 --> 01:00:51.999
When they are working to clean up, first,
that street, to maintain security there-because

01:00:51.999 --> 01:00:56.180
people are-women have been killed and so on,
so-by those people-maintain that security

01:00:56.180 --> 01:01:00.700
there, then distribute food, and so on.

01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:05.800
From the food-we're just kind of elaborating,
not just put in terms-it will be-we cited

01:01:05.800 --> 01:01:07.170
from other examples.

01:01:07.170 --> 01:01:09.059
So idea is to engage them.

01:01:09.059 --> 01:01:13.950
(Inaudible)-but is to really engage them because
they are a majority.

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:17.400
And if they are unemployed, we are creating
future revolutionaries.

01:01:17.400 --> 01:01:24.079
GAVIN: That's a very powerful message about
the importance of inclusion.

01:01:24.079 --> 01:01:29.820
And I hope everyone will join me in thanking
President Geingob.

01:01:29.820 --> 01:01:38.470
It's an incredible opportunity to get to hear
from you in such a conversational setting,

01:01:38.470 --> 01:01:43.980
and I suspect that there are-there's more
than one person around the table mentally

01:01:43.980 --> 01:01:52.259
making plans to visit Namibia and learn more,
or return to Namibia and be delighted once

01:01:52.259 --> 01:01:53.259
again.

01:01:53.259 --> 01:01:54.259
So thank you so very much.

01:01:54.259 --> 01:01:55.259
We wish you all the best into the future.

01:01:55.259 --> 01:01:56.259
(Applause.)

01:01:56.259 --> 01:01:57.259
GEINGOB: Thank you very much.

01:01:57.259 --> 01:01:58.259
Thank you.

01:01:58.259 --> 01:01:59.259
(Applause.)

01:01:59.259 --> 01:01:59.265
(END)
This is an uncorrected transcript.

